# Chickpea Tikka Masala

Based on [https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/…](https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/food/chickpea-tikka-masala-its-not-traditional-but-it-sure-tastes-great/2017/07/24/d6727f58-6e37-11e7-96ab-5f38140b38cc_story.html) ([wayback](http://web.archive.org/web/20240625035635/https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/food/chickpea-tikka-masala-its-not-traditional-but-it-sure-tastes-great/2017/07/24/d6727f58-6e37-11e7-96ab-5f38140b38cc_story.html))

{ rating="5" image="chickpea_tikka_masala.jpeg" }
:::
:::

## Ingredients

- [ ] 1 [Cauliflower]{ ing="cauliflower" }
- [ ] 1 Tbsp [extra-virgin olive oil]{ ing="extra-virgin-olive-oil" }
- [ ] 1/2 tsp [fine sea salt]{ ing="fine-sea-salt" }
- [ ] 1 medium [yellow onion]{ ing="yellow-onion" }, diced
- [ ] 1/2 tsp [ground ginger]{ ing="ground-ginger" }
- [ ] 3 cloves [garlic]{ ing="garlic" }, minced
- [ ] 1 Tbsp [garam masala]{ ing="garam-masala" } ([../reference/spices_garam_masala.md](../reference/spices_garam_masala.html))
- [ ] 1/4 tsp [ground cayenne pepper]{ ing="ground-cayenne-pepper" }
- [ ] 2x 15 oz can [chickpeas]{ ing="chickpeas" }, rinsed and drained
- [ ] 1 28oz can of [crushed tomatoes]{ ing="crushed-tomatoes" }
- [ ] 3/4 cup [canned coconut milk]{ ing="canned-coconut-milk" } (half of a can)
- [ ] 8 oz bag of [spinach]{ ing="spinach" }
- [ ] 1/2 cup [cilantro]{ ing="cilantro" }, chopped (optional)
- [ ] [Cooked basmati rice]{ ing="cooked-basmati-rice" } or warmed Naan, for serving

## Recipe

1. In an Instant Pot, pour in 1 cup of water and place the [cauliflower]{ ing="cauliflower" } on a trivet. Set cook time to zero and seal the vent. When at pressure, quick release to avoid overcooking. Alternatively, place the florets in a microwave safe bowl with 1-2 Tbsp of water and microwave for four minutes
1. Carefully trim florets from the [Cauliflower]{ ing="cauliflower" } into bite-size pieces and set the leaves aside
1. Rinse the [chickpeas]{ ing="chickpeas" }, mince the [garlic]{ ing="garlic" }, and, if using, start cooking the [rice]{ ing="cooked-basmati-rice" } or prepare to warm the Naan in a cast iron pan or toaster
1. Heat the [oil]{ ing="extra-virgin-olive-oil" } in a large, nonreactive skillet medium heat. Add the [cauliflower]{ ing="cauliflower" } florets, onion, and [salt]{ ing="fine-sea-salt" }. Cook, stirring occasionally, until the [onions]{ ing="yellow-onion" } are soft and translucent and the cauliflower is slightly charred (~7 min)
1. Add the [ginger]{ ing="ground-ginger" } and [garlic]{ ing="garlic" } and cook until fragrant, about 1 minute
1. Add the [garam masala]{ ing="garam-masala" } and additional cayenne. Stir until fragrant (~30s)
1. Add the [tomatoes]{ ing="crushed-tomatoes" }, [chickpeas]{ ing="chickpeas" }, [coconut milk]{ ing="canned-coconut-milk" }, [spinach]{ ing="spinach" }, and [cilantro]{ ing="cilantro" }
1. Increase the heat to medium-high; once the mixture begins bubbling around the edges, reduce the heat to low to maintain a gentle bubbling
1. Cook, stirring occasionally, until the sauce thickens (20 min)
1. Serve on [rice]{ ing="cooked-basmati-rice" } or with a side of naan. Sprinkle with more chopped [cilantro]{ ing="cilantro" } if using

## Notes

1. See a similar [recipe where the cauliflower is roasted](https://www.washingtonpost.com/recipes/tandoori-cauliflower) ([wayback](http://web.archive.org/web/20231231225048/https://www.washingtonpost.com/recipes/tandoori-cauliflower))
